<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kiel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kiel is a port city on the Baltic coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and was the principal base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>'s navy. Its harbour and the Kiel Canal gave it strategic importance. In 1918 a mutiny of sailors at Kiel sparked the revolution that brought down the German Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiel.jpg" alt="Kiel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayuntamiento,_Kiel,_Alemania,_2019-09-10,_DD_96-98_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kiel lies on the Kieler Förde, an inlet of the Bay of Kiel, in the southeast of the Jutland Peninsula, at the mouth of the Schwentine River and about 90 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The Kiel Canal, which joins the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, has one end in the district of Holtenau and the other at Brunsbüttel. Most of the city belongs to Holstein, while the districts north of the canal are historically part of Southern Schleswig.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Count Adolf IV of Holstein founded the city in 1233 as Holstenstadt tom Kyle, and his eldest son, John I of Schauenburg, granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> city rights in 1242. As capital of the county, later the duchy, of Holstein, it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> from 1284 until it was expelled in 1518 for harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. Kiel University was founded in 1665 by Christian Albert, Duke of Holstein-Gottorp.</p><p>From 1773 to 1864 the town belonged to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, who ruled Holstein through a personal union. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864 it was administered briefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 led to its annexation by Prussia. King William I moved Prussia's Baltic fleet there on 24 March 1865, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s main naval base the city grew from 18,770 people in 1864 to about 200,000 in 1910.</p><p>In late 1918 the fleet stationed at Kiel was ordered to sail for a last battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. The sailors, who thought it a suicide mission, refused, took over Kiel and spread the revolt to other northern ports, setting off the revolution that ended the monarchy and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> heavy bombing destroyed more than 80 per cent of the remaining old town. British forces seized the city on 5 May 1945, and in 1946 it was named capital of the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kiel is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Its oldest building is the 13th-century Church of St. Nicholas. The shipyard Howaldtswerke-Deutsche Werft, founded in 1838, built the first German submarine, the Brandtaucher, in 1850.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kiel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German revolution, begun by the sailors' mutiny at <b>Kiel</b>, reached Berlin on 9 November 1918, when Max von Baden announced the abdication of Wilhelm II as Emperor and King of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2024 study from <b>Kiel</b> University found that inequality was lower in the mega-sites, and that its return after several generations may explain their abandonment.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The county repeatedly divided into lines such as Holstein-<b>Kiel</b>, Holstein-Plön and Holstein-Rendsburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li><li><blockquote>German nationalists in the duchies responded by forming a provisional government at <b>Kiel</b> on 24 March, led by Wilhelm Hartwig Beseler and Prince Frederick of Noer, claiming both duchies for the German Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-schleswig-holstein">Provisional Government of Schleswig-Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
